I’ll keep everything safe and educational by using a placeholder payload.

Placeholder payload: SECRET_VARIANT

ROT13 encoding
- Original: SECRET_VARIANT
- ROT13: FRPERG_INEVNAG

Verification
- Apply ROT13 again to FRPERG_INEVNAG and you should recover SECRET_VARIANT.

Quick examples you can try yourself

- Python (uses built-in rot13 via codecs)
  payload = "SECRET_VARIANT"
  rot13_version = __import__("codecs").decode(payload, 'rot_13')
  print(rot13_version)  # FRPERG_INEVNAG
  assert __import__("codecs").decode(rot13_version, 'rot_13') == payload

- JavaScript (manual ROT13 implementation)
  function rot13(s) {
    return s.replace(/[A-Za-z]/g, function(c) {
      const code = c.charCodeAt(0);
      const base = (code >= 65 && code <= 90) ? 65 : 97;
      return String.fromCharCode(((code - base + 13) % 26) + base);
    });
  }
  const payload = "SECRET_VARIANT";
  const encoded = rot13(payload);
  console.log(encoded); // FRPERG_INEVNAG
  const decoded = rot13(encoded);
  console.assert(decoded === payload);

Notes
- ROT13 is a reversible, simple letter substitution. It is not a security mechanism; it’s purely a formatting/obfuscation exercise.
- Non-letter characters (like underscores) remain unchanged in ROT13.
- If you want to explore more variations, we can run ROT13 on other safe placeholders like "TEST_ENV" or "DUMMY_TOKEN" and verify round-trips similarly.

